<h2 id="problems">Common speaker problems and what to do</h2>
<p>Most muffled phone speakers fall into a handful of everyday situations. Knowing which one you are dealing with helps you pick the right mode and decide whether sound alone is enough.</p>
<h3>Caught in the rain</h3>
<p>Rain usually leaves a thin film of fresh water over the bottom grille. Shake the phone gently with the speaker facing down, dry the outside, then run the <a href="/water-eject/">water eject tone</a> once. Fresh rainwater evaporates cleanly, so one or two passes plus an hour of air drying normally restores full sound.</p>
<h3>Steam from the shower or kitchen</h3>
<p>Taking a phone into a steamy bathroom lets warm vapour condense inside the grille and even behind it. The sound may seem fine at first and turn dull later as droplets gather. Move the phone to a cooler, dry room, wait ten minutes for condensation to settle, then run the auto clean cycle. Avoid doing this repeatedly, because steam can work past seals that resist splashes.</p>
<h3>Pocket lint and fluff</h3>
<p>Lint builds up slowly, so the speaker gets quieter over weeks rather than suddenly. Use a soft, dry brush to lift fibres from the surface first, then run the <a href="/dust-cleaner/">dust cleaner</a> to shake loose any particles caught in the mesh. Sound cannot pull out a tightly packed plug of lint, so the brush step matters.</p>
<h3>Sand after a beach day</h3>
<p>Sand grains are heavier than dust and often mixed with salt water. Tap the phone lightly against your palm with the speaker facing down, brush the grille, and only then use sound. If the phone went into the sea, follow your manufacturer's advice about rinsing with fresh water before trying any tone, since dried salt can corrode contacts.</p>
<h3>Muffled calls but normal music</h3>
<p>If music sounds fine but the person on a call sounds distant, the problem is probably the earpiece speaker at the top of the screen, not the main speaker. Flip the phone so the earpiece faces down and run the water eject or <a href="/vibration-cleaner/">vibration cleaner</a> mode. If callers say <em>you</em> sound muffled, the microphone holes are blocked instead; brush them gently, because tones do not affect microphones.</p>
<h3>Only one side sounds wrong</h3>
<p>Phones with stereo speakers can be blocked on one side only. Use the speaker selector above to send the tone to just the left or right channel, and check the result with the <a href="/speaker-test/">speaker test</a>. Hold the phone in landscape so you know which physical speaker is which.</p>
